    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The 'closed-loop' distinction seems a bit thin, though. If the examiner looks closely at 2018-HYD-09, they might argue that a liquid cooling system in that context is inherently closed-loop or that the synergy you mentioned is just an obvious application of existing micro-channel tech to a new cell type. \n\nI'm worried that if we lead with that, they'll hit us with a non-obviousness rejection immediately. Is there anything else in the client's specs or a different way we can frame the distinction to make it airtight? We really can't afford a rejection on this one; the client is expecting a clean pass.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To strengthen your position, focus the distinction on the *material science and interface* rather than just the cooling loop itself. Argue that the integration is not merely embedding, but rather a novel thermal interface layer that actively couples the liquid coolant directly to the functional transport pathways within the separator. Emphasize that the closed-loop nature specifically manages thermal runaway propagation *across* the solid electrolyte boundary in a way prior art does not address. This shifts the focus from general cooling to specific, advanced thermal control for solid-state integrity.",
      "source": "target",
